/*! \page user_field_kgrid_page K-Grid Field Data Format (Periodic)

 \ref user_field_rgrid_page  (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
 \ref scft_page              (Next)
<BR>

The pscf_rp program can also read and write files in a k-grid format 
that contains the unsymmetrized discrete Fourier transform of the field 
associated with each monomer type.  The required file format is very 
similar to the r-grid format used for fields defined on a coordinate 
space grid. The file consists of a header and a data section. 

The format of the header section of a k-grid field file is identical 
to that used for the \ref user_field_rgrid_page "r-grid" coordinate 
space file format. As for the r-grid format, the header includes a 
list of the number of grid points used in each direction, labelled 
by the parameter name "mesh".

The data section contains the Fourier coefficients obtained by a
discrete Fourier transform of each field at wavevectors that are
commensurate with the periodic unit cell. Allowed wavevectors are 
given by
\f[
    \textbf{k}(n_0, \ldots, n_{D-1}) = \sum_{i=0}^{\textrm{D}-1}
    n_{i}\textbf{b}_{i}
\f]
where \f$D\f$ is the dimensionality of the crystal (i.e., dim
in the header file), \f$\textbf{b}_{i}\f$ is a reciprocal lattice 
basis vector, and \f$ n_{0}, \ldots, n_{D-1} \f$ are a set of
integers. 

For a grid with \f$N_{i}\f$ grid points along direction \f$i\f$, the 
data format for the discrete Fourier transform of a real-valued field 
stores Fourier components for values of \f$n_{i}\f$ in the range 
\f$0 \leq n_{i} \leq N_{i} - 1\f$ for indices \f$i < D-1\f$, but for
\f$0 \leq n_{i} \leq N_{i}/2\f$ for the last index, \f$ i = D-1 \f$.
The number of rows in the data section is equal to the total number 
of such wavevectors, and each row contains values of Fourier 
coefficients associated with a single wavevector, with coefficients 
for fields associated with different monomer types in different 
columnns.

Coefficients for different wavevectors are output in sequential
order, using the last index (e.g., \f$n_{2}\f$ for a 3D crystal)
as the most rapidly varying (inner-most) loop index. Reading or
writing of the data section for the Fourier transform of a 3D 
real-valued field is performed by a loop of the form:
\code
   for (n0 = 0; n0 < N0; ++n0) {
      for (n1 = 0; n1 < N1: ++n1) {
         for (n2 = 0; n2 < N2/2; ++n2) {
            [Read or write coefficients for wavevector k(n0, n1, n2)]
         }
      }
   }
\endcode
where N0, N1, and N2 are the number of grid points in directions
0, 1, and 2.

<em> Note </em>: The format of the data section of a k-grid file, 
as described above, is different from the format used in the older
Fortran version of PSCF. In the Fortran version, the number of
gridpoints is halved in the first index (`n0` in the code above)
rather than the last index, as is the case here. The two programs
thus use incompatiable formats for k-grid field files.

<BR>
 \ref user_field_rgrid_page  (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
 \ref user_field_page        (Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
 \ref scft_page              (Next)
*/
